  "warnings": [
    "lineage agent notes: Things to check when matching the PySpark output against the legacy output:\n1) earned_pct = earned_days*100/term_days uses integer division in T-SQL. The value is cut down to a whole number, then stored as DECIMAL(5,2), so values like 33.00 appear, not 33.33.\n2) earned_eur and unearned_eur divide by a fixed 365, not by term_days. For terms longer than 365 days, or when the cover period is fully earned, earned_eur can be more than written_eur, which makes unearned_eur negative. The decimal multiply happens before the divide, so precision follows SQL Server's decimal rules.\n3) daily_rate_eur uses ROUND(..., 4, 1), which cuts the value off at 4 places instead of rounding it.\n4) monthly_instalment_eur divides by term_days/30, which is integer division. If that gives 0 (terms under 30 days), the divisor falls back to 1.\n5) fx_rate takes one of two paths. EUR rows get the constant 1. Non-EUR rows get the latest stg.fx_rates.rate_to_eur on or before txn_date, with ties broken by the higher _row_seq. A non-EUR transaction with no rate on or before its txn_date is dropped by the CROSS APPLY. Currency matching depends on how CHAR(3) values are compared, including trailing spaces.\n6) A transaction whose policy has no current dim_policy row (is_current='Y') is dropped by the inner join. If a policy has more than one current row, the transaction is duplicated and breaks the primary key on txn_id.\n7) The NOLOCK reads can return results that change from run to run.\n8) The code doesn't read stg.premium_transactions._row_seq or stg.fx_rates.source. There is no dedup on txn_id."
  ]
